<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jul 8, 2022">
<CPscript title="Circle Packing Menagerie" level="min" >
<description>This script gives you a short tour through a circle packing menagerie. You will see examples circle packings and some of the available display options. Press 'Next' to go through the examples; open the "Script" frame to read accompanying descriptions.</description>
<text> The is a trip through a menagerie of circles packings in the plane, the sphere, and the hyperbolic plane. Along the way, a few simple display options are illustrated. You can save these packings to your own directory for further use: see 'Save' on the menu bar.  

We have started with a simple "blob" packing, just 21 circles, The next command draws the edges connecting tangent circles and the faces they form, giving the "carrier" of the triangulation. </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" tooltip="start over" iconname="bookmark.png">act 0;Cleanse;infile_read molecule.p;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="button_ok.png">Disp -w -ff -c; </cmd>
<text> This pattern of 80 circles is 'CirclePack's "Owl" mascot. Display options allow us to color and display the "eyes" and boundary circles. </text>
<cmd iconname="centrejust.png">act 1;infile_read Owl.p;Disp -w -c -cfc5 21 22 </cmd>
<cmd iconname="dnd_multi.png">color -c 120 b;color -c 20 21 22;Disp -w -f -cf b 21 22; </cmd>
<text> We can create circle packings on the sphere, as well. The spherical carrier has spherical geodesics (arcs of great circles) as edges, and we have color options. There the "degree" of a circle (the number of neighboring circles in the pattern) is reflected in the color. Grab the sphere with the mouse to rotate it. </text>
<cmd iconname="psi.png">act 2;set_screen -d;infile_read simp_sph.p;Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="xeyes.png">color -c d;Disp -w -cf -f; </cmd>
<text> Circle packings are not all simply connected: the following is a packing of an annulus, as you can see by displaying its carrier with two boundaries curves (in blue). </text>
<cmd iconname="kivio.png">act 0;infile_read annulus.p;scale .9;Disp -w -c </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">Disp -w -c -f -ec5t4 b </cmd>
<text> Next is a "Doyle spiral". Every circle has 6 neighbors. Use the "+" magnifying glass icon to zoom in, and move the packing in the window by dragging it with the first mouse button. Display commands that us 'Disp' (capital D) instead of 'disp' will set the default drawing operation to be used when moving or zooming. </text>
<cmd iconname="mozilla.png">act 1;infile_read mild_spiral.p;Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="netbeans.png">set_vlist 9 8 19 11 3 1 6 17 13 14 15 32 50 51 52;color -c 20 vlist;Disp -w -c -cf vlist </cmd>
<text> We will be forming some packings into rectangles. Note the chain of boundary faces. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">act 2;infile_read simple_rect.p;Disp -w -c -e b; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="kghostview.png">color -f 180 a;Disp -w -c -e b -ff Ra </cmd>
<text> Don't be fooled by the elementary nature of some of these packings. We can work with much larger, more involved packings, as the following two examples show. </text>
<cmd iconname="apollon.png">act 0;infile_read simple_welding.p;Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> Note the time it takes to display larger packings: you need to be careful with your button-pressing, since it is easy to outrun the software. </text>
<cmd iconname="userconfig.png">act 1;infile_read twpent5.p;Disp -w -c; </cmd>
<text> 'CirclePack' can also work with triangulations of compact surfaces. The next example is a torus, which is addressed in Section 6.8 of the book. (Topologically, a torus is the surface of a one-holed donut). The packing looks flat, and that is true locally. However, the color-coding on the edges indicates "side-pairings": thus the circles on the red segment on one side are actually identified (are the same) as the circles on the red segment of the other side. Similarly for the other colored segments. </text>
<cmd iconname="amarok.png">act 2;infile_read torus.p;set_screen -d;layout;Disp -w -C -R a; </cmd>
<text> The "color-wheel" packing shows the colors we have access to in CirclePack. Click the mouse button on one: its index is the code for that color. These codes are used in display calls. For example, enter 'disp -w -cfc233 a' in a "Commands:" line (e.g., under the main canvas) and you will see the screen cleared and all the circles displayed as filled with color 233 (a green). Try it out! </text>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_online.png">act 0;infile_read color-wheel.p;Disp -w -cf; </cmd>
<text> We can manipulate a few animal forms. Owl will become a familiar one: it is useful because the eyes, shape, and symmetry are easy to follow in demonstrations.  Here also is a snake, and the last packing is its "maximal" packing. </text>
<cmd iconname="klines.png">act 0;infile_read Owl.p;set_screen -d;Disp -w -c -cfc20 21 22; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="icq_dnd.png">act 1;set_screen -d;infile_read overlap_snake.p;color -c 100 a;color -c 20 109 110;Disp -w -cf; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="editdelete.png">act 2;set_screen -d;infile_read max_snake.p;color -c 20 109 110;Disp -w -c -cf 109 110; </cmd>
<text> This is just a preliminary tour. Hope you enjoyed it. </text>
  </CPscript>
<CPdata>
    <AboutImage name="AboutImage.jpg">

    </AboutImage>
